      "Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore": "Warning",
      "OpenIddict": "Warning"
    }
  },
  "_AllowedHosts": "Kestrel's Host-header allowlist, semicolon-separated (e.g. \"your-identity-host;www.your-identity-host\"). '*' accepts ANY Host header — combined with a reverse proxy that forwards the client's original Host unchecked, this enables host-header poisoning (spoofed password-reset links, cache-poisoning). Set this to the real production host(s); it does not need to match Issuer's scheme, only its host.",
  "AllowedHosts": "your-identity-host",
  "Sufficit": {
    "Vault": {
      "Enabled": false,
      "RequireEncryptionInProduction": false,
      "KeySource": "dataprotection",
      "DataProtectionPurpose": "Sufficit.Identity.Vault.Master.v1",
      "EnableSecretStore": false,
      "ManageSigningKeys": false,
      "SigningKeyName": "oidc-signing"
    },
    "Identity": {
      "Issuer": "https://your-identity-host/",
      "PublicUrl": "https://your-identity-host/",
      "_PublicOrigin": "Audit preserves request-derived URLs for a rolling upgrade but logs their use. After PublicUrl/Issuer is pinned in every environment, set Mode=Enforce so Host headers can never shape security links or metadata.",
      "PublicOrigin": {
        "Mode": "Audit"
      },
      "ConnectionStringName": "DefaultConnection",
      "_Database": "Schema provisioning policy. In Development the host ALWAYS applies pending EF Core migrations on startup (MigrateAsync). In production it does so ONLY when Database:AutoMigrate=true (default false — conservative for the legacy shared Duende/Skoruba database, which predates the EF model). When AutoMigrate is false in production, provision schema from the checked-in canonical SQL (docs/migration/sql/*) before each deploy. CRITICAL: when enabling AutoMigrate, ALWAYS set AllowedDatabaseNames to the concrete database name(s) the migrator may touch — if the connection string points elsewhere (e.g. the production database by a config typo), the process refuses to start rather than migrate the wrong database. Empty AllowedDatabaseNames disables the guard (Development only).",
      "Database": {
        "_TransportMode": "Compatibility preserves rolling deployments. Set RequireVerifiedTls with SslMode=VerifyCA/VerifyFull and a trusted CA, or PrivateSocket with an explicit UnixSocket for an audited private-lab exception.",
        "TransportMode": "Compatibility",
        "_AutoMigrate": "true applies pending EF migrations on startup outside Development. Default false — flip only after confirming the schema is aligned and AllowedDatabaseNames is set.",
        "AutoMigrate": false,
        "_AllowedDatabaseNames": "Allow-list of database names the automatic migrator may touch (parsed from the connection string). When non-empty, a mismatch fails startup fast. Recommended in production. Empty = no guard (Development only).",
        "AllowedDatabaseNames": [],
        "_ConnectionPool": "Explicit MySQL/MariaDB pool policy applied by the STS. MaximumSize is per process/replica. Size it against the database max_connections budget across every replica and workload.",
        "ConnectionPool": {
          "MaximumSize": 50,
          "MinimumSize": 0,
          "ConnectionTimeoutSeconds": 15,
          "CommandTimeoutSeconds": 30,
          "ConnectionLifetimeSeconds": 180,
          "ConnectionIdleTimeoutSeconds": 180,
          "ResetOnCheckout": true,
          "ApplicationName": "Sufficit.Identity"
        },
        "_Watchdog": "After the startup delay, probes the AppDbContext. Three consecutive failed/timed-out probes stop the process with exit code 1 so systemd or the orchestrator recreates the process and pools. Keep liveness separate: /health remains process-only and /health/ready includes the database.",
        "Watchdog": {
          "Enabled": true,
          "StartupDelaySeconds": 60,
          "ProbeIntervalSeconds": 30,
          "ProbeTimeoutSeconds": 10,
          "ConsecutiveFailuresBeforeRestart": 3
        }
      },
      "_TrustedProxies": "Array of CIDR strings for reverse-proxy networks allowed to set X-Forwarded-* headers. Leaving this UNSET (not listed) trusts only loopback (ASP.NET Core default). The example values below reflect the Sufficit production topology (verified 2026-07-21 from nginx access logs on eveo-apps/apoint-apps/castrum-apps): 203.0.113.10 (eveo-cloud Proxmox gateway), 203.0.113.11 (apoint-cloud gateway), plus the internal sufflocal/healthcheck networks 10.0.0.0/8 and the AWS Route53 health-checker range 198.51.100.0/24. If TrustedProxies is empty in production, the rate limiter partitions by the proxy's IP (one shared bucket for the whole deployment — self-inflicted 429s) and X-Forwarded-Proto is dropped (Request.Scheme stays http, defeating the cookie Secure policy). Adjust per environment in appsettings.<env>.json.",
      "TrustedProxies": [
        "203.0.113.10/32",
        "203.0.113.11/32",
        "10.0.0.0/8",
        "198.51.100.0/24"
      ],
      "Security": {
        "_FailClosedOnInsecureDefaults": "Set true only after the production posture findings have been resolved or explicitly acknowledged. Leaving this false during the staged rollout preserves service availability while the CSP and management policies are calibrated.",
        "FailClosedOnInsecureDefaults": false
      },
      "Management": {
        "Enabled": false,
        "RoutePrefix": "api",
        "RequireAuthorization": true,
        "RequiredScope": "identity.management",
        "Authorization": {
          "ObjectAccess": {
            "Mode": "Observe",
            "ContextClaimType": "identity_context",
            "LegacyContextId": "global"
          },
          "ProtectedPrincipals": {
            "Mode": "Observe",
            "TierClaimType": "identity_principal_tier",
            "ProtectedUserIds": [],
            "ProtectedRoles": [],
            "BreakGlassClaimType": "identity_break_glass",
            "BreakGlassClaimValue": "identity.management"
          }
        },
        "_RequireMfa": "true requires the access token to carry an amr claim proving multi-factor auth (item 5.2 [L3]) for the management endpoints. Default true; explicitly set false only for an isolated migration environment.",
        "RequireMfa": true
      },
      "_Scim": "Optional SCIM 2.0 provisioning endpoint at /scim/v2. Groups are opaque provisioning groups and are never interpreted as ASP.NET Identity roles or Sufficit business authorities.",
      "Scim": {
        "Enabled": false,
        "_RequireAuthorization": "Deprecated compatibility alias. When RequireScope is omitted, this controls only the scope check and never disables authentication or the client allow-list.",
        "RequireAuthorization": true,
        "RequireScope": true,
        "RequireAllowedClient": true,
        "ClientPolicyMode": "Enforce",
        "RequiredScope": "scim",
        "_RequireMfa": "true requires every SCIM request to carry an amr claim proving MFA (RFC 8176), mirroring the management API. Default false — SCIM is typically driven by machine-to-machine provisioning (client_credentials), which cannot do MFA. Enable only when SCIM is exposed to delegated (human) flows; otherwise restrict the scim scope to a single dedicated provisioning client.",
        "RequireMfa": false,
        "_AllowedClientIds": "Allow-list of OAuth client_id values permitted to call SCIM (M4, eval). SCIM is FULL-DIRECTORY-TRUST: any allowed client can enumerate every user, read PII, reset any password and delete any account. Default empty = FAIL-CLOSED (SCIM stays inaccessible until at least one trusted provisioning client is listed). Add only dedicated, narrowly-scoped provisioning clients; never a general-purpose application client.",
        "AllowedClientIds": [],
